00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,760
 Alright question number one.

18
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:39,840
 An eight-year-old patient presents with an LS Class 2 fracture of tooth number eight.

19
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:47,000
 In an effort to attain a popal diagnosis, which of the following tests is least reliable?

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,600
 So go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

21
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:59,360
 Alright, so for each question, let's break it down and start with what we know.

22
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:07,080
 So you may recall an LS Class 2 fracture involves both the enamel and dentin layers, but that's

23
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:14,160
 actually not relevant to the question being asked, which is asking about diagnostic testing.

24
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:21,640
 And so we know vitality testing is unreliable for two to eight weeks following a traumatic

25
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,160
 injury.

26
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,720
 So we could use that information to narrow our choices down to either A or B, because

27
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:33,240
 C and D aren't vitality tests, while A and B are.

28
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:41,320
 And if you recall, EPT or electric pulp test is by the numbers the least reliable vitality

29
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,600
 testing method we have.

30
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,040
 So that's all there is to it.

31
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,000
 The answer is B.

32
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,660
 So for the board exam, remember that percussion is generally regarded as the most reliable

33
00:02:54,660 --> 00:03:01,640
 testing method while EPT is generally regarded as the least reliable method.

34
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:05,960
 Alright, question number two.

35
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:12,120
 Radiographic examination shows a horizontal fracture of the apical third of the root.

36
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,560
 The best treatment is which of the following?

37
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,640
 So go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

38
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:27,960
 Alright, so horizontal fracture, like with perforations, the more apically they happen,

39
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:30,840
 the better the prognosis.

40
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:37,080
 So the fact that it's apical may clue you in that we'd like a more conservative treatment

41
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:43,480
 option, eliminating some of the less conservative options like extraction and apicalectomy that

42
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:45,640
 are little too extreme.

43
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:53,480
 And then a pexification along with a pexogenesis are for immature permanent teeth, not root

44
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:54,480
 fractures.

45
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:57,880
 So we can eliminate that choice as well.

46
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:00,780
 So we're left with A or B.

47
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:05,320
 Now if you remember with horizontal fractures, we really had two routes.

48
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:09,880
 If the tooth was vital, we'd want to splint as soon as possible.

49
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:14,760
 If the tooth was necrotic, we'd have to do a root canal treatment.

50
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:20,920
 But vitality isn't provided here in the question, and we won't be able to receive a good vitality

51
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:24,960
 measurement until about two to eight weeks after trauma.

52
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:30,920
 So the best answer here, since we don't know vitality, the tooth is the more conservative

53
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:35,840
 and comprehensive option, which is B.

54
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:38,680
 So A is definitely tempting.

55
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:46,720
 But the best answer is B, because say in eight weeks time, the tooth becomes necrotic.

56
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:50,780
 At that point, we can inform the patient and recommend the ideal treatment, which would

57
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:53,800
 then be a root canal treatment.

58
00:04:53,800 --> 00:05:00,040
 But if the tooth were ultimately vital, doing a premature root canal treatment before we

59
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:05,000
 could gather reliable evidence would be a poor treatment option.

60
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:13,360
 All right, so question number three, purple anatomy dictates which are the following

61
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:17,160
 access openings for a maxillary first molar.

62
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:21,440
 Go ahead and pause the video, and then we'll talk about this.

63
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:27,320
 All right, so I love questions like these, because they're nice, cut, and dry.

64
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:32,640
 We don't need to know any of the nitty gritty details about the exact specifications of

65
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:36,360
 the access prep, just what shape it is.

66
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:41,740
 And honestly, guys, the majority of questions on the exam are kind of like this.

67
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:47,160
 If you know the high yield facts really well, which is always what I stress, you'll do really

68
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:49,120
 well on the exam.

69
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:54,600
 So here's an example of just being able to know the high yield facts.

70
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:02,120
 So a maxillary first molar, which commonly has three canals, the ideal access is a blunted

71
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:09,080
 triangle or rhomboidal shape, providing straight line access for those three canals.

72
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:14,480
 So the answer here is C.

73
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:20,040
 All right, question number four, symptomatic irreversible pulpitus pain in which of the

74
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:25,600
 following sites is most likely to radiate to the ear.

75
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:29,840
 Go ahead and pause the video, and then we'll talk about this.

76
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:37,120
 All right, so this question is referencing the concept of referred pain, which we talked

77
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:42,320
 about in our first video, where tooth pain can actually be felt in other areas of the

78
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:43,820
 body.

79
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:49,720
 We talked about innervation of the teeth and face and briefly the nerve map associated

80
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:51,640
 with that.

81
00:06:51,640 --> 00:07:00,600
 And so the trap answer here is maxillary molar, because it's closest anatomically to the ear.

82
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:07,400
 But actually, pre or regular pain often refers from mandibular molars.

83
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:15,760
 And that's because they both share V3 innervation, as you can see with this nerve map here.

84
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:24,000
 And so the answer here is D. Mandibular molars of these four are most likely to radiate pain

85
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,400
 to the ear.

86
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:30,680
 All right, question number five.

87
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:35,880
 So before I read this question, I'm actually going to jump to an informative slide, because

88
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:42,760
 like I alluded to before, unfortunately, this topic of cracked tooth syndrome is something

89
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:47,400
 basically a sort of miscellaneous, and I didn't really have time to cover it in the previous

90
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:48,400
 videos.

91
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:53,040
 So I'm going to very quickly now, because knowing this information can definitely net

92
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:57,480
 you at least one to two questions on the exam.

93
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:05,760
 So cracked tooth syndrome is basically this syndrome collection of signs and symptoms

94
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:10,280
 that are associated with having a crack in a tooth.

95
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:17,960
 So there's usually pain on biting and on release, but usually only when the patient bites a

96
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:25,200
 very certain way on a certain cusp, let's say, and pain is usually more associated with

97
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:32,240
 the actual release of pressure rather than increased biting force.

98
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:38,680
 There's also sometimes sensitivity to cold and or hot, but the syndrome sometimes completely

99
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:45,800
 asymptomatic and the patient doesn't feel any symptoms associated with the crack.

100
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:51,720
 Now cracks usually extend deep into dentin and propagate mesiodistally.

101
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:57,760
 As you can see in this image very clearly, often on the marginal bridge of a posterior

102
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:00,960
 tooth.

103
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:08,040
 Dies and translimination are more helpful than radiographs, because radiographs can easily

104
00:09:08,040 --> 00:09:11,920
 miss this crack, which occurs along one plane.

105
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:18,000
 The radiograph isn't taken at multiple different angulations, like a root fracture, you won't

106
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,200
 be able to see it.

107
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:23,440
 So radiographs aren't very helpful here.

108
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:29,480
 You can also use a tooth sleuth on each cusp to aid in location of the crack.

109
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:35,680
 So that would be using something like this tooth sleuth here, and you put this rounded

110
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:42,560
 tip on each cusp and have the patient bite down and see either on biting or on release

111
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:48,240
 or both see if a certain cusp is really bothering them, and then that can help you along with

112
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:53,240
 using colored dyes and translimination, lighting through the tooth.

113
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,560
 You can see where that crack is.

114
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:04,120
 If the pulp is healthy, you can splint the tooth with like an orthoband and observe or

115
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:10,720
 eventually crown that tooth, or the pulp is diseased, bacteria and byproducts have leaked

116
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:11,720
 into the pulp.

117
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:17,520
 For instance, through this crack, conservative root canal treatment and restoration with

118
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:23,000
 a crown because the tooth is a posterior tooth, would be recommended.

119
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:28,960
 Alright so now that all that information is nice and fresh, we can revisit the question.

120
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:34,480
 So which of the following statements most likely applies to a cracked tooth?

121
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:41,080
 Go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

122
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:47,720
 Alright so nice and fresh the information since it was on the last slide and we can just

123
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:49,400
 kind of go right along here.

124
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:56,120
 So the direction of the crack, it's either mesiodistal or facial lingual most likely,

125
00:10:56,120 --> 00:11:01,880
 and you'll know it's mesiodistally because it's occurring along those, across those marginal

126
00:11:01,880 --> 00:11:05,240
 ridges of posterior teeth, just like in the picture.

127
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:11,400
 It's a very classic representation of a cracked tooth syndrome.

128
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:17,640
 And radiographic examination is not the best way to detect a cracked tooth using the dyes

129
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:23,120
 or transillumination, or even the tooth sleuth can be much more valuable than radiographs,

130
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:25,640
 which can easily miss a crack.

131
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:33,760
 So the answer of these is A, only.

132
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:42,280
 Alright question number six, what is the clinical hallmark of a chronic peri-ridicular abscess?

133
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:48,080
 Go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

134
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:55,240
 Alright so first of all, there may be some words that look confusing or unfamiliar.

135
00:11:55,240 --> 00:12:00,640
 In this question or in any question that you get on the board exam, and my single best advice

136
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:07,520
 for you is, always take a deep breath and start with what you know.

137
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:10,900
 Remember you know more than you may think you do.

138
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:17,960
 The question is asking about the characteristic clinical feature of a chronic peri-ridicular

139
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:19,960
 abscess.

140
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:23,200
 Well maybe I don't remember that exactly.

141
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,880
 I don't remember this chronic peri-ridicular abscess, it doesn't sound familiar.

142
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:35,560
 But I do remember there was an acute apical abscess and chronic apical abscess, and well

143
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:44,240
 peri-ridicular means around the root, similar to meaning apical, and you can kind of go

144
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:49,440
 through this thought process, it's very similar looking to chronic apical abscess, and in

145
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:55,760
 fact this is absolutely synonymous with a chronic apical abscess.

146
00:12:55,760 --> 00:13:00,120
 And once you know that, you may know the one defining feature that we talked about with

147
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:04,120
 a chronic apical abscess is a sinus tract.

148
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:09,120
 And so the hallmark is simply sinus tract drainage, the one with sinus tract in the

149
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:10,480
 answer.

150
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:17,200
 And the answer for this question is B, and there you go.

151
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:22,640
 Alright question number seven, which of the following tissues will not regenerate after

152
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,720
 a root canal treatment?

153
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:31,120
 So go ahead, pause the video, and then we'll talk about this.

154
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:34,280
 Alright so this is kind of a fun question I think.

155
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:39,680
 We never really talked about this directly, but we did cover all the information you need

156
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:44,280
 to know in the pulp biology section.

157
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:49,840
 So remember root canal treatment is sort of like a pulpectomy where we remove all of the

158
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:55,240
 pulp tissue followed by cleaning, shaping, and then obturation and filling.

159
00:13:55,240 --> 00:14:01,880
 So we completely remove the pulp tissue which contains odontoblasts.

160
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:08,200
 Now without odontoblasts, the tooth can no longer make dentin, like the tertiary dentin

161
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:12,720
 that we stimulate to form with calcium hydroxide.

162
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:17,880
 The other three tissues can regenerate with a healing response because the cells that

163
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:20,760
 make them are still intact.

164
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:26,400
 But since odontoblasts are thrown away with the pulp tissue, the tooth loses its ability

165
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:33,080
 to create dentin, so the answer is A.

166
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:38,680
 Alright question number eight, while performing non-surgical endodontic therapy, you detect

167
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:40,200
 a ledge.

168
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:41,960
 What should you do?

169
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:48,400
 Go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this question.

170
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:54,400
 Alright so ledgeing was one of the procedural complications we had talked about, and a ledge

171
00:14:54,400 --> 00:15:02,680
 is an artificial irregularity created on the surface of a root canal wall.

172
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:07,400
 So let's go through the answer choices, I'm going to skip over A for a second, B, fill

173
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:13,400
 as far as you have reamed, well B would be leaving part of the root canal apical to the

174
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:21,800
 ledge completely untreated, which could absolutely lead to failure, leaving bacteria in the root

175
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:23,760
 canal untreated.

176
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:31,000
 So that's not a great option, using a small round burr and remove the ledge, well certainly

177
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:37,360
 sounds nice, but using a round burr to remove a ledge is really impractical if not impossible

178
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:41,400
 when working in such a confined space.

179
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:47,240
 And how about D, continue working gently to remove the ledge?

180
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:51,880
 So this answer choice is definitely enticing.

181
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:57,080
 But continuing to do what you're doing to remove a ledge no matter how gentle you may

182
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:03,000
 be will only make things worse, you're just feeding into that ledge that you've created

183
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:09,000
 artificially with the file, and you're not following the natural curve of the canal.

184
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:16,920
 So definitely the ideal method here of the options is to use a smaller file to renegotiate

185
00:16:16,920 --> 00:16:22,880
 the natural canal, and you can maybe even place a curve in the file like we talked about to

186
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:27,560
 try and avoid the ledge, but this can be very technically challenging.

187
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:36,400
 So the answer is A, and we want to bypass the ledge by maybe curving it or certainly using

188
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:42,320
 a smaller instrument to try to get past this ledge that we created and follow the natural

189
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:47,440
 canal.

190
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:53,320
 All right, so before I read this question, I'm going to go on to the topic of vertical

191
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:58,840
 root fracture, which again is another miscellaneous topic I wasn't able to cover in the previous

192
00:16:58,840 --> 00:16:59,840
 videos.

193
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:05,440
 So I definitely wanted to make sure I got a chance to cover it for you all now.

194
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:13,640
 So vertical root fracture starts apically and progresses coronaly in terms of its general

195
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:15,920
 development as a lesion.

196
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:24,560
 So it can occur after a cementation of a post or most commonly excessive condensation forces

197
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:27,080
 on an overprepared canal.

198
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:33,920
 So when you condense the gut aperture too forcefully, that's the number one iatrogenic

199
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:39,880
 cause of a vertical root fracture.

200
00:17:39,880 --> 00:17:44,600
 When you probe around the tooth with a vertical root fracture, it says here isolated probing

201
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:50,600
 defect at the site of the fracture, what that means is everything basically seems normal

202
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:56,200
 within normal limits, and then you have this sudden drop of the perioprobe to like a 10

203
00:17:56,200 --> 00:18:01,680
 millimeter pocket, let's say, and seems totally out of the ordinary.

204
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:07,640
 That's because the pocket is going down to where the vertical root fracture has extended

205
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:09,680
 to apically.

206
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:15,240
 And so the isolated probing defect is right at the site of the fracture.

207
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:23,520
 It's accompanied by often a J-shaped or teardrop, radiolucency, and this is a sinister thing.

208
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:29,160
 Vertical root fracture is definitely not something that you want for your patients.

209
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:34,800
 For single rooted tooth, really the only thing we can do for this tooth, it's a hopeless

210
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:39,360
 prognosis, really can't save it, and we have to extract the tooth.

211
00:18:39,360 --> 00:18:45,520
 If it's a multi rooted tooth, we can either extract the tooth or a bit more conservatively

212
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:51,160
 just resect the affected root with the fracture in it.

213
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:54,560
 So now that we know all of this, we can revisit our question.

214
00:18:54,560 --> 00:19:00,320
 The ideal treatment option for a vertical root fracture is which of the following?

215
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:06,720
 So go ahead and pause the video and then we'll talk about this.

216
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:12,880
 All right, so the moral of this story here is if you see the words vertical root fracture,

217
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:18,160
 or if you are given clues that heavily suggest a vertical root fracture, this would be the

218
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:21,720
 time to pick extraction as your answer.

219
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:27,520
 The vertical root fracture, think extraction on the exam.

220
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:34,640
 All right, question number 10, which of the following conditions indicates a periodontal

221
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:39,360
 problem rather than an endodontic problem exists?

